116.642	1794663262.646	{"type":"text","timestamp":1794663262645,"sessionID":"ses_e25bd9bd5ffeOUp7vBFsfW88R2","part":{"id":"prt_1da44179b001F17YI76echIfo9","messageID":"msg_1da42650c001116qZxMiq1ORk5","sessionID":"ses_e25bd9bd5ffeOUp7vBFsfW88R2","type":"text","text":"## 6×6 domino tilings\n\n### Method 1: Kasteleyn formula\n\nFor an even \\(m\\times n\\) rectangle,\n\n\\[\nT(m,n)=\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\\prod_{k=1}^{n}\n\\left(4\\cos^2\\frac{\\pi j}{m+1}+4\\cos^2\\frac{\\pi k}{n+1}\\right)^{1/4}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(m=n=6\\), set\n\n\\[\na_j=4\\cos^2\\frac{\\pi j}{7}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a_{7-j}=a_j\\), the multiset is\n\\(\\{a_1,a_2,a_3,a_1,a_2,a_3\\}\\). Grouping equal factors gives\n\n\\[\nT(6,6)=(2a_1)(2a_2)(2a_3)\\bigl[(a_1+a_2)(a_1+a_3)(a_2+a_3)\\bigr]^2.\n\\]\n\nNow put \\(x_j=2\\cos(2\\pi j/7)\\). Then \\(a_j=2+x_j\\), and\n\\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\) are the roots of\n\n\\[\np(x)=x^3+x^2-2x-1.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=1}^3 2a_j\n=8\\prod_{j=1}^3(2+x_j)\n=8\\bigl(-p(-2)\\bigr)\n=8(1)=8.\n\\]\n\nAlso \\(x_1+x_2+x_3=-1\\), so for \\(i<j\\),\n\n\\[\na_i+a_j=4+x_i+x_j=3-x_k,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(x_k\\) is the remaining root. Hence\n\n\\[\n(a_1+a_2)(a_1+a_3)(a_2+a_3)\n=\\prod_{k=1}^3(3-x_k)\n=p(3)=27+9-6-1=29.\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nT(6,6)=8\\cdot 29^2=8\\cdot 841=6728.\n\\]\n\n### Method 2: Transfer-matrix / profile DP\n\nUse a \\(6\\)-bit mask recording cells already covered from the previous row. There are \\(2^6=64\\) states. The row transfer \\(T_{\\text{in},\\text{out}}=1\\) exactly when:\n\n- \\(\\text{out}\\subseteq \\overline{\\text{in}}\\), and\n- the remaining free cells \\(\\overline{\\text{in}\\cup\\text{out}}\\) split into adjacent pairs, so they can be tiled horizontally.\n\nStarting with the empty profile and taking six row transfers,\n\n\\[\nN=(T^6)_{0,0}.\n\\]\n\nCarrying out this 64-state DP gives the \\(6\\times n\\) sequence\n\n\\[\n1,\\ 1,\\ 13,\\ 41,\\ 281,\\ 1183,\\ 6728,\n\\]\n\nso for \\(n=6\\),\n\n\\[\nN=6728.\n\\]\n\nBoth independent methods agree.\n\n---\n\n## Spanning trees of the \\(5\\times5\\) grid\n\nLet \\(G=P_5\\square P_5\\), with \\(N=25\\) vertices. By the Matrix Tree Theorem,\n\n\\[\n\\tau(G)=\\frac{1}{N}\\prod_{\\lambda\\ne 0}\\lambda,\n\\]\n\nover the nonzero Laplacian eigenvalues.\n\nThe path \\(P_5\\) has Laplacian eigenvalues\n\n\\[\n\\mu_j=2-2\\cos\\frac{\\pi j}{5},\\qquad j=0,1,2,3,4.\n\\]\n\nFor the Cartesian product,\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{j,k}=\\mu_j+\\mu_k\n=4-2\\cos\\frac{\\pi j}{5}-2\\cos\\frac{\\pi k}{5}.\n\\]\n\nLet\n\n\\[\nc_j=2\\cos\\frac{\\pi j}{5}.\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\nc_0=2,\\quad c_1=\\varphi,\\quad c_2=\\frac1\\varphi,\\quad\nc_3=-\\frac1\\varphi,\\quad c_4=-\\varphi,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varphi=(1+\\sqrt5)/2\\).\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\tau(G)=\\frac1{25}\n\\prod_{(j,k)\\ne(0,0)}(4-c_j-c_k).\n\\]\n\nLet \\(D=\\{\\varphi,1/\\varphi,-1/\\varphi,-\\varphi\\}\\). The excluded pair is \\((2,2)\\). Partitioning the product:\n\n\\[\nQ=\\left[\\prod_{d\\in D}(2-d)\\right]\n\\left[\\prod_{c\\in D}\\prod_{d\\in\\{2\\}\\cup D}(4-c-d)\\right].\n\\]\n\nThe first factor is\n\n\\[\nA=\\prod_{d\\in D}(2-d)=5.\n\\]\n\nThe second is\n\n\\[\nA\\cdot H,\\qquad\nH=\\prod_{c,d\\in D}(4-c-d).\n\\]\n\nSo\n\n\\[\nQ=A^2H=25H,\n\\]\n\nand therefore\n\n\\[\n\\tau(G)=\\frac{Q}{25}=H.\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(H\\). Write \\(x=\\varphi\\), \\(y=1/\\varphi\\). Then\n\n\\[\nx+y=\\sqrt5,\\qquad x-y=1,\\qquad xy=1.\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal contribution is\n\n\\[\n(4-2x)(4+2x)(4-2y)(4+2y)\n=(12-4x)(12+4y)=80.\n\\]\n\nThe off-diagonal pairs contribute\n\n\\[\n16\\cdot16\\cdot(4-\\sqrt5)^2\\cdot9\\cdot25\\cdot(4+\\sqrt5)^2\n=16\\cdot16\\cdot9\\cdot25\\cdot121\n=6\\,969\\,600.\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nH=80\\cdot 6\\,969\\,600=557\\,568\\,000.\n\\]\n\nSo\n\n\\[\n\\tau(P_5\\square P_5)=557\\,568\\,000.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Final answer:**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{6×6 domino tilings }=6728}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{5×5 grid spanning trees }=557\\,568\\,000}\n\\]","time":{"start":1794663258011,"end":1794663262643}}}
